.SH NAME
messq-quickstart - run the five-minute tour on a throwaway daemon


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBmessq quickstart [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Run the guided tour: a throwaway daemon in a temp directory that you
publish to, ack from, nak with backoff, and deliberately let a message
time out into the DLQ — ending with the unprompted trace of the dead
message. The tour runs real commands, and prints exactly what it runs.

.PP
Nothing outside the tour's own directory is touched, and every MESSQ_*
variable from your environment is ignored: the tour never talks to your
real daemon. No config file, no --data-dir, no setup — that is the point.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for quickstart

.PP
\fB--keep\fP[=false]
	keep the throwaway data dir and print the command that reopens it

.PP
\fB--no-banner\fP[=false]
	skip the banner (for asciinema recordings)

.PP
\fB--output\fP="text"
	output mode: text|ndjson — ndjson emits one record per step

.PP
\fB--pause\fP[=false]
	wait for Enter between steps (default: only on a terminal)


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB-a\fP, \fB--addr\fP="unix:///run/messq/messq.sock"
	daemon address (unix://, http://, https://)

.PP
\fB--color\fP="auto"
	colour: auto|always|never

.PP
\fB--full-ids\fP[=false]
	print full ULIDs instead of abbreviated ones

.PP
\fB-q\fP, \fB--quiet\fP[=false]
	suppress narration; data and errors unaffected

.PP
\fB--timeout\fP=30s
	per-request deadline (0 = none)

.PP
\fB--token-file\fP=""
	file holding the bearer token, mode 0600

.PP
\fB-v\fP, \fB--verbose\fP[=0]
	narrate (-vv dumps each HTTP exchange and the resolved config)


.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
  messq quickstart
  messq quickstart --output ndjson   # one record per step, for machines
  messq quickstart --keep            # keep the data dir and say where it is
.EE


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBmessq(1)\fP


